Access Control (Delegation Framework) {#chapter-access-control}
============================================================

## Scope and Principles ## {#section-access-principles}

RWP deliberately defines no mandatory authorisation model. Data security and information protection are delegated to the implementing systems. This chapter defines an **optional, interoperable delegation framework** that compliant systems MAY implement.

The core principle: access control in RWP is **attribute-based** (ABAC) and **delegatable**. Rights are not assigned to roles in a fixed hierarchy, but expressed as verifiable claims attached to DIDs.

A denied access is not an integrity result. How a denial is communicated is defined in [Validation Response Codes](#subsection-integrity-validation-responsecode).


## Access Policy Block ## {#section-access-policy}

An implementation claiming this optional capability MAY carry the `accessPolicy` block as an entry in the [=Record=]'s `metadata.extensions` array (see [metadata.extensions](#section-metadata-extensions)), using the reserved type identifier `did:rwp:recordweb.org:extension-access-policy`:

<pre class='example json' title='accessPolicy'>
{
  "type": "did:rwp:recordweb.org:extension-access-policy",
  "accessPolicy": {
    "visibility": "restricted",
    "readAccess": [
      "did:rwp:bern.ch:unit-legal",
      "did:rwp:bern.ch:unit-planning"
    ],
    "writeAccess": [
      "did:rwp:bern.ch:user-petra-muster"
    ],
    "delegationAllowed": true,
    "expiresAt": null
  }
}
</pre>

<table class="data">
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Field</th><th>Required</th><th>Description</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>visibility</code></td><td>SHOULD</td><td><code>public</code>, <code>restricted</code>, or <code>confidential</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>readAccess</code></td><td>MAY</td><td>Array of DIDs with read permission</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>writeAccess</code></td><td>MAY</td><td>Array of DIDs with write permission (draft state only)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>delegationAllowed</code></td><td>MAY</td><td>Whether listed DIDs may further delegate access</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>expiresAt</code></td><td>MAY</td><td>ISO 8601 timestamp after which access policy expires</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

An implementation MUST NOT require another implementation to support this extension unless both claim the applicable access-control capability.

## Trust anchor ## {#section-access-trust}
RWP does not define trust in a signing party. For a globally resolvable DID, the namespace is resolved through the GNR to the DID Resolver responsible for it. The DID document and the verification methods obtained from this resolver are authoritative for the DID. For a locally scoped DID, the authoritative resolver is determined by local configuration. Neither the registration of a namespace in the GNR nor the conformance of an AgentRecord to the Root AgentRecord Schema establishes the identity or the authority of the Agent. These remain subject to the trust policy of the verifier. A Proof by a locally scoped DID has meaning only in the scope in which its local resolution is trusted.

A resolver operator can present a DID document history that is internally consistent. A verifier that needs protection against this SHOULD retain previously verified DID document versions.


## Delegation Chain ## {#section-access-delegation}

When `delegationAllowed: true`, a listed DID MAY issue a signed delegation token granting access to a third DID. The delegation token MUST:

- be signed by the delegating DID's verification key
- reference the original Record DID
- specify the access level being delegated (`read` or `write`)
- include an expiry timestamp

Implementing systems MAY enforce delegation chains and MUST validate delegation token signatures before granting access.


## Services and Access Control ## {#section-access-services}

A `service` entry in a DID document (see [Service Entries](#section-didoc-service)), such as a restricted view or a function registry, is a declaration of availability. It does not grant access and does not bypass any access policy. Authorisation for using a declared service or function remains governed by this chapter and by the access policy of the implementing system.

